WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.850
We tried an app that listens to

2
00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:05.150
pupils read aloud and scores them.

3
00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:09.040
Overall it was right nine times in ten.

4
00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.870
But pupils from the village were

5
00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:13.590
marked down far more often.

6
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.800
Today: how a model can be good on average

7
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.080
and still unfair to some pupils.

8
00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:24.270
A model is biased when its mistakes fall

9
00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.050
more on one group than another.

10
00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:29.190
The average can look fine while one

11
00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:30.910
group carries most of the errors.

12
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.940
So we check every group separately.

13
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.880
Split the test pupils by group and work

14
00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:39.580
out the error rate for each one.

15
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.360
If one group's rate is much higher,

16
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:44.600
the model is not fair to them,

17
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:46.740
whatever the overall score says.

18
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.160
Here are the app's errors for

19
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.200
each group of pupils.

20
00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.730
Town pupils had six wrong scores per

21
00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.470
hundred, while village pupils had twenty

22
00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:57.730
two.

23
00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:01.390
Most bias starts in the data.

24
00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:04.170
The app learned from recordings of

25
00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.810
town voices, so village accents

26
00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.350
were rare in its training.

27
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.580
A model can only be as fair as the

28
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:12.680
examples it learned from.

29
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.380
We did three things.

30
00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:18.310
We stopped using the app's score for any

31
00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:21.130
decision, we sent the company recordings

32
00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:24.170
from our village pupils, and a teacher now

33
00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:26.190
listens whenever the app gives a low

34
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:26.430
score.

35
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:28.130
Quick check!

36
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.520
The app is right ninety

37
00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.020
percent of the time overall.

38
00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:35.150
Does that show it is fair to

39
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:36.470
every group of pupils?

40
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:38.140
No.

41
00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:40.550
A good average can hide one

42
00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:42.450
group with many more errors.

43
00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.230
You only see bias when you check

44
00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:46.410
every group separately.

45
00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.210
In the app, open the bias lab.

46
00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:52.900
Change how many examples of each

47
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:54.940
group are in the training data, and

48
00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:56.500
watch each group's error rate.

49
00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:58.210
Your turn.

50
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:02.000
Where does most bias in a model start?

51
00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:04.830
In the data it learned from.

52
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.100
Remember: averages hide unfairness, so

53
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.000
compare errors group by group, fix the

54
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.220
data, and keep a person in the loop.

55
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.200
Try it in class tomorrow: give groups a

56
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.520
made-up table of a model's results for

57
00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.400
boys and girls, or two classes.

58
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:24.940
Ask them to work out the error

59
00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.060
rate for each group and decide

60
00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.360
whether the model is fair.
